)=35-3x-9y$ decreases as $x$ increases, and\n\n- $f(x,y)=35-3x-9y$ decreases as $y$ increases.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14072435/assets/ce0d90127e1f14654f4b32a933f1eb6e76e054c03f19ca40c3f04116aa3c2c93.png)\n\nAs a result, the *maximum* value of $f$ in each subregion is attained when we have the *smallest* possible $x$ and the *smallest* possible $y.$ This is the value at the *bottom left corner* of each subregion. \n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14072435/assets/5b9967c814e46914e0409854de0960e85339adc9b9fd9693677c8e048c342702.png)\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{M}_{ij} & =f({x}_{i-1},{y}_{j-1})=35-3{x}_{i-1}-9{y}_{j-1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nfor all $1\\le i\\le m$ and $1\\le j\\le n.$\n\nFinally, the upper Riemann sum is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}U(f,P) & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{m}{\\sum }_{j=1}^{n}(35-3{x}_{i-1}-9{y}_{j-1})Δ{x}_{i}Δ{y}_{j}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the missing expression is $35-3{x}_{i-1}-9{y}_{j-1}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14072435/state.json" "file_sha256" "06c0ee19ab86b5aed06adc4228a7233ec9d5a0640b4cc5c65c00930a8c8969de"} {"question" "q-75339" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14072435/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "18eb505175667323fde6f9af69c08706940547b7d0442dc38309563563f6e641"} {"question" "q-75339" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14072435/state.json" "file_sha256" "06c0ee19ab86b5aed06adc4228a7233ec9d5a0640b4cc5c65c00930a8c8969de"} {"