j}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere ${M}_{ij}$ is the *maximum* value of $f$ in each subregion ${R}_{ij}.$ \n\nWe consider an arbitrary subregion ${R}_{ij}$ of the partition. This subregion lies between ${x}_{i-1}$ and ${x}_{i}$ over the $x$-axis and ${y}_{i-1}$ and ${y}_{i}$ over the $y$-axis, as shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14072435/assets/88ffe30ae5401e00502b35d851b216768aad69bf94236561617adf23e1ce058f.png)\n\nNotice that:\n\n- $f(x,y)=y-3x+2$ decreases as $x$ increases, and\n\n- $f(x,y)=y-3x+2$ increases as $y$ increases.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14072435/assets/ffa57d3e7fed08212f12d4b9d7c2e0a4d33a3aac71c8b8a03d1a9a7364484deb.png)\n\nAs a result, the *maximum* value of $f$ in each subregion is attained when we have the *smallest* possible $x$ and the *largest* possible $y.$ This is the value at the *top left corner* of each subregion.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14072435/assets/74d546d91e145efea49b70af56cb472e8bbbd1417725cd7fa92a3a278cad75ea.png)\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{M}_{ij} & =f({x}_{i-1},{y}_{j})={y}_{j}-3{x}_{i-1}+2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nfor all $1\\le i\\le m$ and $1\\le j\\le n.$\n\nFinally, the upper Riemann sum is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}U(f,P) & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{m}{\\sum }_{j=1}^{n}({y}_{j}-3{x}_{i-1}+2)Δ{x}_{i}Δ{y}_{j}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the missing expression is ${y}_{j}-3{x}_{i-1}+2.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14072435/state.json" "file_sha256" "06c0ee19ab86b5aed06adc4228a7233ec9d5a0640b4cc5c65c00930a8c8969de"} {"question" "q-75060" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14072435/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "18eb505175667323fde6f9af69c08706940547b7d0442dc38309563563f6e641"} {"question" "q-75060" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14072435/state.json" "file_sha256" "06c0ee19ab86b5aed06adc4228a7233ec9d5a0640b4cc5c65c00930a8c8969de"} {"question" "q-75060" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "3{y}_{j}-{x}_{i-1}+2"] ["math" "{y}_{j-1}-3{x}_{i-1}+2"] ["math" "{y}_{j-1}-{x}_{i}"] ["math" "{y}_{j}-3{x}_{i-1}+2"] ["math" "{y}_{j}-{x}_{i-1}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14072435/state.json" "file_sha256" "06c0ee19ab86b5aed06adc4228a7233ec9d5a0640b4cc5c65c00930a8c8969de"} {"question" "q-75060" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "{y}_{j}-3{x}_{i-1}+2"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14072435/state.json" "file_sha256" "06c0ee19ab86b5aed06adc4228a7233ec9d5a0640b4cc5c65c00930a8c8969de"} {"question" "e-6248" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Let $R = [0,2]\\times [1,3]$ be a region in the $xy$-plane, and let $f(x,y) = x+2y.$ Further, let $P$ be a partition of $R,$ where\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}_{1}={{x}_{0},{x}_{1},…,{x}_{m}},\\,{P}_{2}={{y}_{0},{y}_{1},…,{y}_{n}},\\,P={P}_{1}\\times {P}_{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWrite down the upper Riemann sum $U(f, P).$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14072435/example-6248.json" "file_sha256" "b41af1e97701dcb0e6849f608232999228a736701a719cff89f650dff6b72694"} {"question" "e-6248" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The upper Riemann sum is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}U(f,P) & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{m}{\\sum }_{j=1}^{n}{M}_{ij}Δ{x}_{i}Δ{y}_{j}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $M_{ij}$ is the *maximum* value of $f$ in each subregion $R_{ij}.$ \n\nWe consider an arbitrary subregion $R_{ij}$ of the partition. This subregion lies between $x_{i-1}$ and $x_i$ over the $x$-axis and $y_{i-1}$ and $y_i$ over the $y$-axis, as shown below. \n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14072435/assets/88ffe30ae5401e00502b35d851b216768aad69bf94236561617adf23e1ce058f.png)\n\nNotice that\n\n- $f(x,y) = x+2y$ increases as $x$ increases, and\n\n- $f(x,y) = x+2y$ increases as $y$ increases.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14072435/assets/67daa328cc6ae4cb37be386c327fb4548844fad2adc10476bdf1147c5a5d4218.png)\n\nAs a result, the *maximum* value of $f$ in each subregion is attained when we have the *largest* possible $x$ and the *largest* possible $y.$ This is the value at the *top right corner* of each subregion. \n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14072435/assets/6e58954fccd74eaf67c95df818c4c9050c35b324c5fffe4902a366cb295b8845.png)\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{M}_{ij} & =f({x}_{i},{y}_{j})={x}_{i}+2{y}_{j}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nfor all $1\\leq i\\leq m$ and $1\\leq j\\leq n.$\n\nFinally, the upper Riemann sum is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}U(f,P) & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{m}{\\sum }_{j=1}^{n}({x}_{i}+2{y}_{j})Δ{x}_{i}Δ{y}_{j}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14072435/example-6248.json" "file_sha256" "b41af1e97701dcb0e6849f608232999228a736701a719cff89f650dff6b72694"} {"question" "e-6250" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Let $R = [0,1] \\times [0,2]$ be a region in the $xy$-plane, and let $f(x,y) = 90-x^2-y^2.$\nFurther, let $P$ be a partition of $R,$ where\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}_{1}={{x}_{0},{x}_{1},…,{x}_{m}},\\,{P}_{2}={{y}_{0},{y}_{1},…,{y}_{n}},\\,P={P}_{1}\\times {P}_{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWrite down the upper Riemann sum $U(f, P).$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14072435/example-6250.json" "file_sha256" "c0d56310ae4149a58a3ceb5459361b1e6f7da6745d16b10f9dc4400e41a3c689"} {"question" "e-6250" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The upper Riemann sum is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}U(f,P) & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{m}{\\sum }_{j=1}^{n}{M}_{ij}Δ{x}_{i}Δ{y}_{j}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $M_{ij}$ is the *maximum* value of $f$ in each subregion $R_{ij}.$ \n\nWe consider an arbitrary subregion $R_{ij}$ of the partition. This subregion lies between $x_{i-1}$ and $x_i$ over the $x$-axis and $y_{i-1}$ and $y_i$ over the $y$-axis, as shown below. \n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14072435/assets/88ffe30ae5401e00502b35d851b216768aad69bf94236561617adf23e1ce058f.png)\n\nNotice that\n\n- $f(x,y) = 90-x^2-y^2$ decreases as $x$ increases on $[0,1],$ and\n\n- $f(x,y) = 90-x^2-y^2$ decreases as $y$ increases on $[0,2].$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14072435/assets/ce0d90127e1f14654f4b32a933f1eb6e76e054c03f19ca40c3f04116aa3c2c93.png)\n\nAs a result, the *maximum* value of $f$ in each subregion is attained when we have the *smallest* possible $x$ and the *smallest* possible $y.$ This is the value at the *bottom left corner* of each subregion. \n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14072435/assets/5b9967c814e46914e0409854de0960e85339adc9b9fd9693677c8e048c342702.png)\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{M}_{ij} & =f({x}_{i-1},{y}_{j-1})=90-{x}_{i-1}^{2}-{y}_{j-1}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nfor all $1\\leq i\\leq m$ and $1\\leq j\\leq n.$\n\nFinally, the upper Riemann sum is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}U(f,P) & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{m}{\\sum }_{j=1}^{n}(90-{x}_{i-1}^{2}-{y}_{j-1}^{2})Δ{x}_{i}Δ{y}_{j}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14072435/example-6250.json" "file_sha256" "c0d56310ae4149a58a3ceb5459361b1e6f7da6745d16b10f9dc4400e41a3c689"} {"question" "e-9495" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Let $R = [2,4] \\times [0,1]$ be a region in the $xy$-plane, and let $f(x,y) = 2x - y.$ Further, let $P$ be a partition of $R,$ where\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}_{1}={{x}_{0},{x}_{1},…,{x}_{m}},\\,{P}_{2}={{y}_{0},{y}_{1},…,{y}_{n}},\\,P={P}_{1}\\times {P}_{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWrite down the upper Riemann sum $U(f,P).$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14072435/example-9495.json" "file_sha256" "7209de39c3cb99616c05f51c63f1981cc79d4639ca563d30e828ba185612937e"} {"question" "e-9495" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The upper Riemann sum is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}U(f,P) & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{m}{\\sum }_{j=1}^{n}{M}_{ij}Δ{x}_{i}Δ{y}_{j}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $M_{ij}$ is the *maximum* value of $f$ in each subregion $R_{ij}.$ \n\nWe consider an arbitrary subregion $R_{ij}$ of the partition. This subregion lies between $x_{i-1}$ and $x_i$ over the $x$-axis and $y_{i-1}$ and $y_i$ over the $y$-axis, as shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14072435/assets/88ffe30ae5401e00502b35d851b216768aad69bf94236561617adf23e1ce058f.png)\n\nNotice that\n\n- $f(x,y) = 2x - y$ increases as $x$ increases, and\n\n- $f(x,y) = 2x - y$ decreases as $y$ increases.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14072435/assets/01b291ca7cd81b88cf6ed06fdcdb2ffcbb1f78255d0f57d496823d347c2fb0b3.png)\n\nAs a result, the *maximum* value of $f$ in each subregion is attained when we have the *largest* possible $x$ and the *smallest* possible $y.$ This is the value at the *bottom right corner* of each subregion.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14072435/assets/de36b521dea1f925754a9c630c3d4a2c405c087a191138f1eaaa5771becb8c21.png)\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{M}_{ij} & =f({x}_{i},{y}_{j-1})=2{x}_{i}-{y}_{j-1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nfor all $1\\leq i\\leq m$ and $1\\leq j\\leq n.$\n\nFinally, the upper Riemann sum is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}U(f,P) & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{m}{\\sum }_{j=1}^{n}(2{x}_{i}-{y}_{j-1})Δ{x}_{i}Δ{y}_{j}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14072435/example-9495.json" "file_sha256" "7209de39c3cb99616c05f51c63f1981cc79d4639ca563d30e828ba185612937e"}] "usage" {"q-75060" [] "e-6248" [] "q-75056" [] "e-9495" [] "e-6250" [] "q-75065" []} "basis" 2654 "retractions" [[17592186180209 :question/problem "Let $R = [2, 4] \\cdot [2, 4]$ be a region in the $xy$-plane, and let $f(x, y) = 2x + y + 1$.\nFurther, let $P$ be a partition of $R$, where\n\n$P_{1} = {x_{0}, x_{1}, …, x_{m}}, P_{2} = {y_{0}, y_{1}, …, y_{n}}, P = P_{1} \\cdot P_{2}$.\n\nGiven that the upper Riemann sum of $f$ with respect to $P$ is given by\n$U(f, P) = ∑_(i = 1)^(m)∑_(j = 1)^(n)AAAAAΔx_{i}Δy_{j}$\nwhere $Δx_{i} = x_{i} - x_{i - 1}$ and $Δy_{j} = y_{j} - y_{j - 1}$, what is the missing expression in the double summation above?"] [17592186180206 :question/problem "Let $R = [15, 20] \\cdot [3, 5]$ be a region in the $xy$-plane, and let $f(x, y) = x - 3y$.\nFurther, let $P$ be a partition of $R$, where\n\n$P_{1} = {x_{0}, x_{1}, …, x_{m}}, P_{2} = {y_{0}, y_{1}, …, y_{n}}, P = P_{1} \\cdot P_{2}$.\n\nGiven that the upper Riemann sum of $f$ with respect to $P$ is given by\n$U(f, P) = ∑_(i = 1)^(m)∑_(j = 1)^(n)AAAAAΔx_{i}Δy_{j}$\n\nwhere $Δx_{i} = x_{i} - x_{i - 1}$ and $Δy_{j} = y_{j} - y_{j - 1}$,\nwhat is the missing expression in the double summation above?"] [17592186180207 :question/problem "Let $R = [- 3, 2] \\cdot [4, 7]$ be a region in the $xy$-plane, and let $f(x, y) = y - 3x + 2$.\nFurther, let $P$ be a partition of $R$, where\n\n$P_{1} = {x_{0}, x_{1}, …, x_{m}}, P_{2} = {y_{0}, y_{1}, …, y_{n}}, P = P_{1} \\cdot P_{2}$.\n\nGiven that the upper Riemann sum of $f$ with respect to $P$ is given by\n$U(f, P) = ∑_(i = 1)^(m)∑_(j = 1)^(n)AAAAAΔx_{i}Δy_{j}$\n\nwhere $Δx_{i} = x_{i} - x_{i - 1}$ and $Δy_{j} = y_{j} - y_{j - 1}$, what is the missing expression in the double summation above?"] [17592186179452 :question/problem "Let $R = [0,2]\\times [1,3]$ be a region in the $xy$-plane, and let $f(x,y) = x+2y$. Further, let $P$ be a partition of $R$, where\n\n$P_{1} = {x_{0}, x_{1}, …, x_{m}}, P_{2} = {y_{0}, y_{1}, …, y_{n}}, P = P_{1} \\cdot P_{2}$.\n\nWrite down the upper Riemann sum $U(f, P)$."] [17592186179452 :question/worked-solution "The upper Riemann sum is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nU(f, P) &= ∑_(i = 1)^(m)∑_(j = 1)^(n)M_{ij}Δx_{i}Δy_{j}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $M_{ij}$ is the *maximum* value of $f$ in each subregion $R_{ij}$.\n\nWe consider an arbitrary subregion $R_{ij}$ of the partition. This subregion lies between $x_{i-1}$ and $x_i$ over the $x$-axis and $y_{i-1}$ and $y_i$ over the $y$-axis, as shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/3696/Source/Images/edad92d80f1137da0db424cf1d10358c.png)\n\nNotice that\n\n- $f(x,y) = x+2y$ increases as $x$ increases, and\n- $f(x,y) = x+2y$ increases as $y$ increases.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/3696/Source/Images/963f3198c226e0fcf8688c291d514773.png)\n\nAs a result, the *maximum* value of $f$ in each subregion is attained when we have the *largest* possible $x$ and the *largest* possible $y$. This is the value at the *top right corner* of each subregion.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/3696/Source/Images/6870b71405e9ec1c7bd8f7feb11f4f90.png)\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nM_{ij} &= f(x_{i}, y_{j}) = x_{i} + 2y_{j}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nfor all\n\n$$\n1\\leq i\\leq m\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n1\\leq j\\leq n\n$$\n\nFinally, the upper Riemann sum is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nU(f, P) &= ∑_(i = 1)^(m)∑_(j = 1)^(n)(x_{i} + 2y_{j})Δx_{i}Δy_{j}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186179453 :question/problem "Let $R = [0,1] \\times [0,2]$ be a region in the $xy$-plane, and let $f(x,y) = 90-x^2-y^2$.\nFurther, let $P$ be a partition of $R$, where\n\n$P_{1} = {x_{0}, x_{1}, …, x_{m}}, P_{2} = {y_{0}, y_{1}, …, y_{n}}, P = P_{1} \\cdot P_{2}$.\n\nWrite down the upper Riemann sum $U(f, P)$."] [17592186179453 :question/worked-solution "The upper Riemann sum is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nU(f, P) &= ∑_(i = 1)^(m)∑_(j = 1)^(n)M_{ij}Δx_{i}Δy_{j}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $M_{ij}$ is the *maximum* value of $f$ in each subregion $R_{ij}$.\n\nWe consider an arbitrary subregion $R_{ij}$ of the partition. This subregion lies between $x_{i-1}$ and $x_i$ over the $x$-axis and $y_{i-1}$ and $y_i$ over the $y$-axis, as shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/3696/Source/Images/f0dc3e5ada5e449af8a26059d5ef0f8c.png)\n\nNotice that\n\n- $f(x,y) = 90-x^2-y^2$ decreases as $x$ increases on $[0,1]$, and\n- $f(x,y) = 90-x^2-y^2$ decreases as $y$ increases on $[0,2]$.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/3696/Source/Images/3bc69da4098b8e380e39e0546cdce312.png)\n\nAs a result, the *maximum* value of $f$ in each subregion is attained when we have the *smallest* possible $x$ and the *smallest* possible $y$. This is the value at the *bottom left corner* of each subregion.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/3696/Source/Images/fde88ca2195c884141658dc35168ef03.png)\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nM_{ij} &= f(x_{i - 1}, y_{j - 1}) = 90 - x_{i - 1}^{2} - y_{j - 1}^{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nfor all\n\n$$\n1\\leq i\\leq m\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n1\\leq j\\leq n\n$$\n\nFinally, the upper Riemann sum is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nU(f, P) &= ∑_(i = 1)^(m)∑_(j = 1)^(n)(90 - x_{i - 1}^{2} - y_{j - 1}^{2})Δx_{i}Δy_{j}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186179519 :question/problem "Let $R = [2,4] \\times [0,1]$ be a region in the $xy$-plane, and let $f(x,y) = 2x - y$. Further, let $P$ be a partition of $R$, where\n$P_{1} = {x_{0}, x_{1}, …, x_{m}}, P_{2} = {y_{0}, y_{1}, …, y_{n}}, P = P_{1} \\cdot P_{2}$.\n\nWrite down the upper Riemann sum $U(f,P)$."] [17592186179519 :question/worked-solution "The upper Riemann sum is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nU(f, P) &= ∑_(i = 1)^(m)∑_(j = 1)^(n)M_{ij}Δx_{i}Δy_{j}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $M_{ij}$ is the *maximum* value of $f$ in each subregion $R_{ij}$.\n\nWe consider an arbitrary subregion $R_{ij}$ of the partition. This subregion lies between $x_{i-1}$ and $x_i$ over the $x$-axis and $y_{i-1}$ and $y_i$ over the $y$-axis, as shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/3696/Source/Images/a1b215e94fff6e51b4665cd869553c5c.png)\n\nNotice that\n\n- $f(x,y) = 2x - y$ increases as $x$ increases, and\n- $f(x,y) = 2x - y$ decreases as $y$ increases.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/3696/Source/Images/78607f0271a92fd1540c9ddf593f0010.png)\n\nAs a result, the *maximum* value of $f$ in each subregion is attained when we have the *largest* possible $x$ and the *smallest* possible $y$. This is the value at the *bottom right corner* of each subregion.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/3696/Source/Images/c7d415e021fb7f02f6aa222664e0ab60.png)\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nM_{ij} &= f(x_{i}, y_{j - 1}) = 2x_{i} - y_{j - 1}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nfor all\n\n$$\n1\\leq i\\leq m\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n1\\leq j\\leq n\n$$\n\nFinally, the upper Riemann sum is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nU(f, P) &= ∑_(i = 1)^(m)∑_(j = 1)^(n)(2x_{i} - y_{j - 1})Δx_{i}Δy_{j}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"]] "immutable_answers" [17592186179112 17592186179113 17592186179114 17592186179115 17592186179116 17592186179118 17592186179119 17592186179120 17592186179121 17592186179122 17592186179130 17592186179131 17592186179132 17592186179133 17592186179134] "immutable_fields" [[17592186179111 :answer-field/correct] [17592186179111 :answer-field/id] [17592186179111 :answer-field/key] [17592186179111 :answer-field/type] [17592186179117 :answer-field/correct] [17592186179117 :answer-field/id] [17592186179117 :answer-field/key] [17592186179117 :answer-field/type] [17592186179129 :answer-field/correct] [17592186179129 :answer-field/id] [17592186179129 :answer-field/key] [17592186179129 :answer-field/type]] "no_history" []}
